            you generally sacrifice compared to $1,500+ machines is
            touchscreen size, top-tier deck cushioning systems, and longer
            warranty coverage on parts and labor.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
            What's the most important spec to check on a budget treadmill?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed m-0">
            Continuous horsepower (CHP) matters more than peak horsepower
            marketing numbers. Look for at least 2.0-2.5 CHP for walking and
            light jogging, and 2.75-3.0 CHP if you plan to run. Weight
            capacity relative to your household's heaviest user is the
            second most important factor, since machines rated closer to
            their true limit tend to wear out faster.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
            Are folding treadmills less durable than non-folding ones?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed m-0">
            Not necessarily, but they do introduce a hinge point that can
            develop play or rattle over years of use if the mechanism is low
            quality. Full-size non-folding treadmills like the Schwinn 810
            tend to feel more rigid during use, but a well-built folding
            machine like the Horizon T101 held up just as well in our
            long-term testing.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
            Do I need an iFit or app subscription to use a budget connected
            treadmill?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed m-0">
            No, machines like the ProForm Carbon T7 and NordicTrack T Series
            6.5 S work in manual mode without a subscription, but you lose
            most of the value proposition, since the touchscreen becomes a
            basic stat display. If you don't want an ongoing subscription
            cost, a non-connected machine like the Horizon T101 or Schwinn
            810 makes more financial sense long term.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
            How long should a treadmill under $1,000 realistically last?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed m-0">
            With proper maintenance, lubrication, and a dedicated outlet, a
            well-built budget treadmill typically lasts 5-7 years of regular
            household use. Machines pushed beyond their rated duty cycle, run
            without belt lubrication, or shared across a household exceeding
            the weight rating tend to fail closer to the 2-3 year mark.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
